diff --git a/forth/file-sysc4.4 b/forth/file-sysc4.4
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..212f6a9
--- /dev/null
+++ b/forth/file-sysc4.4
@@ -0,0 +1,27 @@
+\ file-sysc4.4 - fsys 4: file-sysc3.4's single-SYSCALL I/O with the user
+\ output buffered where Gforth buffers it (Iteration 207, the owner). The
+\ standard leaves buffering open (FLUSH-FILE implies it); Gforth's practice
+\ and its proposal: never buffer the user input device; never buffer the user
+\ output device when it is a terminal; buffer it when it is not - a pipe, a
+\ file (complang.tuwien.ac.at/forth/ansforth-cvs/buffering.html). So WRITE
+\ asks once whether stdout is a terminal (ioctl TCGETS): there it writes at
+\ once, as fsys 3; elsewhere it adds to a 4 KB buffer on the heap - the
+\ dictionary holds only its [address, fill], registered with the engine
+\ (SYSCALL -5), whose t_flush empties it wherever the native engine flushed
+\ its own: before every system call, before a read blocks, at every exit and
+\ fault. A full buffer is flushed by SYSCALL -6. Input stays unbuffered.
+
+CREATE FS-OB 0 , 0 ,                             \ the buffer's address, its fill
+VARIABLE FS-OMODE  0 FS-OMODE !                  \ 0 not yet asked, 1 buffered, 2 a terminal: direct
+4096 CONSTANT FS-OSIZE
+: FS-OINIT ( --- )
+  FS-OSIZE FS-MMAP DUP 0< IF DROP 2 FS-OMODE ! EXIT THEN
+  DUP FS-OB ! 0 FS-OB CELL+ !
+  1 21505 ROT 16 FS3                             \ ioctl(1, TCGETS, the buffer as scratch): 0 on a terminal
+  0= IF 2 ELSE 1 FS-OB -5 FS1 DROP THEN FS-OMODE ! ;
+: WRITE ( c-addr u --- )
+  FS-OMODE @ 0= IF FS-OINIT THEN
+  FS-OMODE @ 2 = IF 1 FS-WRITE DROP EXIT THEN
+  DUP FS-OSIZE > IF -6 FS0 DROP 1 FS-WRITE DROP EXIT THEN
+  DUP FS-OB CELL+ @ + FS-OSIZE > IF -6 FS0 DROP THEN
+  FS-OB @ FS-OB CELL+ @ + SWAP DUP FS-OB CELL+ +! CMOVE ;
diff --git a/lab/evolve/README.md b/lab/evolve/README.md
index 0e00a53..0c32030 100644
--- a/lab/evolve/README.md
+++ b/lab/evolve/README.md
@@ -515,3 +515,20 @@ the handler that was gone. Measured: the smallest 1,217 bytes smaller in all
 (fsys 2: 628), the fastest 1,921; the I/O workloads slower - the kernel
 workload +37-89%, the corpus +23-88% - fib and bubble not; the standard suite
 14 of 14 as s6. Evolution weighs 0 to 3.
+
+## fsys 4 (Iteration 207): the single-SYSCALL family with the output buffered
+
+The owner: a buffered-output level for evolution, with tests that buffering
+breaks nothing. The standard leaves buffering open; Gforth's practice and
+proposal (complang.tuwien.ac.at/forth/ansforth-cvs/buffering.html): never
+buffer input; never buffer output to a terminal; buffer it to a pipe or a
+file. fsys 4 is fsys 3 with WRITE so (forth/file-sysc4.4): it asks once
+(ioctl TCGETS) - on a terminal it writes at once, elsewhere into 4 KB on the
+heap whose [address, fill] the engine knows (SYSCALL -5), so t_flush empties
+it wherever the native engine flushed its own: before every system call,
+before a read blocks, at every exit and fault. lab/evolve/outbuf-check.py
+tests it against native: stdout and stderr on one pipe (ten programs, byte
+for byte; a signal death where native loses output the levels keep), the
+prompt shown before a blocking read, and a terminal shown at once (native
+holds it). The workloads print little: fsys 4 is about fsys 3's speed - their
+cost is READ-LINE's scan and system calls a line, not the output.
